bclassification for communicable- and injuries-associated deaths were not reported; only aggregated information was provided. AIDS, acquired immunodeficiency syndrome; HIV, human immunodeficiency virus; STDs, sexually transmitted diseases. The number of annual deaths increased from 2604 in 2017 to 3919 in 2021. Compared to 2017, the total number of deaths increased by 13.9% in 2018, 38.2% in 2019, 33.1% in 2020 and 50.5% in 2021. This was mainly driven by an increase of 220.2% and 306.6% in communicable diseases–associated deaths (mostly attributable to COVID-19) during 2020 and 2021 ( Figure 1A ). More deaths occurred during the winter months between June and August ( n = 4612, 27.7%) than in other seasons. Over a third of communicable diseases–associated deaths ( n = 1375, 34.0%) were recorded in winter, likely due to the surge in COVID-19 cases in 2020 and 2021 ( Figure 1B ). The supplementary material depicts country-specific annual ( supplementary material S5 ) and monthly ( supplementary material S6 ) trends of deaths among international travellers. Argentina reported the most substantial decrease (74.0% from 2017 to 2021) in deaths over time, and Chile and Peru experienced the greatest increase (270 and 101% from 2017 to 2021, respectively). Figure 1. Open in a new tab Total number of deaths among international travellers in South America from 2017 to 2021 (A) by year and (B) by month-year. Notes: Ill-defined diseases (unknown) cause of death were excluded. Information about the origin of the traveller was available in 8622 records (50.1% of total 17 245 travellers). A significant proportion of travellers originated from South American countries ( n = 6773, 78.6%), with the highest number of deaths recorded among travellers from Venezuela ( n = 3546, 41.1%). Travellers from North America and the Caribbean represented 10.2% of the deaths, while those from Europe and Asia accounted for 7.8% and 2.4%, respectively (see Figure 2 ). Figure 2. Open in a new tab Network maps of the distribution of the countries of origin of travellers who died in South America from 2017 to 2021. Notes: Data on the country of origin of travellers who died in Chile and Uruguay were not available. Specific causes of deaths Cardiovascular diseases caused the highest number of deaths overall ( n = 3454, 20.0%). Ecuador ( n = 76, 31.2%) and Uruguay ( n = 117, 30.1%) reported the highest proportion of cardiovascular diseases–associated deaths, while Peru ( n = 234, 15.4%) reported the lowest. Malignant neoplasms were the second most common cause of death overall ( n = 2690, 15.6%). Chile reported the highest number and proportion of deaths due to malignant neoplasms ( n = 1755, 22.0%), while Ecuador reported the lowest ( n = 10, 4.2%). In all other countries, cardiovascular diseases were the leading cause of death, with the exception of Chile, where malignant neoplasms were the most common cause (see Table 1 ). Respiratory infections were the leading cause of death among the communicable diseases and the third most common cause of death overall ( n = 2257, 13.1%). Peru ( n = 386, 25.5%) reported the highest proportion of deaths due to respiratory infections. These were the most common cause of communicable diseases–associated deaths in all countries, except for Brazil, where ‘other infectious diseases’ ( n = 197, 9.1%) and perinatal infections ( n = 193, 8.9%) were more frequently recorded ( Table 1 ). Intentional injuries, including violence ( n = 727, 4.2%), self-inflicted injuries ( n = 77, 0.5%) and other intentional injuries ( n = 9, 0.1%) accounted for more than one in four deaths (26.0%) due to injuries and together ( n = 813, 4.7%) were the fourth most common cause of death overall. In Colombia, intentional injuries accounted for 59.0% of all injury-related deaths and 14.0% of all deaths, making them the second leading cause of death after cardiovascular diseases. In Brazil, intentional injuries ( n = 169) accounted for 38.5% of injury-associated deaths. In South America, motor vehicle accidents ( n = 501, 16%) were the second most common cause of death due to injuries. In Argentina ( n = 113), motor vehicle accidents accounted for 42.0% of all reported injury-associated deaths and were the fourth most common cause of death (8.5%). In Ecuador, motor vehicle accidents ( n = 37) accounted for 36.0% of all injury associated deaths and were the second most common cause of death (15.4%) after cardiovascular diseases. Drowning was an uncommon cause of injury-related death in South America (0.6%), yet it represented 8.7% of all fatalities in Ecuador, ranking as the third most common cause of death in the country ( Table 1 ). There were other important differences in causes of death between countries. The proportion of communicable diseases–associated deaths ranged from 30.5% in Peru to 6.2% in Uruguay. Injury-associated deaths were reported more frequently than communicable diseases–associated deaths in Ecuador (42.5 vs 7.1%), Uruguay (24.5 vs 6.2%) and Argentina (19.9 vs 15.6%) ( Table 1 ). The tree maps in supplementary material S7 show the distribution and breakdown of the causes of death by country. Predictors for causes of death Sociodemographic variables Overall, most deaths recorded in travellers occurred in men ( n = 10 265, 61.7%) and among those aged >85 years ( n = 2136, 13%). Half of the deaths were recorded in travellers >55–59 years of age. Most reported deaths occurred in travellers from upper-middle (67.5%)- or high (23.1%)-income countries ( Table 2 ). Country-specific demographic characteristics and causes of death in travellers are available in supplementary material S8 . Table 2. Sociodemographic characteristics by causes of deaths among international travellers in South America from 2017 to 2021 ( N = 16 678 a ) Variable Communicable Injuries NCDs Total χ 2 -test b χ 2 -test b χ 2 -test b N = 4041 N = 3112 N = 9477 N = 16 678 C vs I C vs N I vs N n (%) n (%) n (%) n (%) P -value P -value P -value Sex at birth Male 2460 (60.94) 2488 (79.95) 5317 (56.11) 10 265 (61.74) <0.001 <0.001 <0.001 Female 1577 (39.06) 624 (20.05) 4159 (43.89) 6360 (38.26) <0.001 <0.001 <0.001 Age group (years) <20 598 (15.17) 303 (9.79) 414 (4.39) 1315 (7.98) <0.001 <0.001 <0.001 20–24 93 (2.36) 506 (16.34) 122 (1.29) 721 (4.38) <0.001 <0.001 <0.001 25–29 179 (4.54) 528 (17.05) 202 (2.14) 909 (5.52) <0.001 <0.001 <0.001 30–34 243 (6.17) 445 (14.37) 307 (3.25) 995 (6.04) <0.001 <0.001 <0.001 35–39 241 (6.12) 312 (10.08) 335 (3.55) 888 (5.39) <0.001 <0.001 <0.001 40–44 268 (6.8) 230 (7.43) 419 (4.44) 917 (5.57) <0.001 <0.001 <0.001 45–49 226 (5.73) 177 (5.72) 481 (5.1) 884 (5.37) 0.863 0.216 0.183 50–54 231 (5.86) 147 (4.75) 591 (6.26) 969 (5.88) 0.063 0.247 0.002 55–59 273 (6.93) 112 (3.62) 734 (7.78) 1119 (6.79) <0.001 0.045 <0.001 60–64 299 (7.59) 77 (2.49) 835 (8.85) 1211 (7.35) <0.001 0.007 <0.001 65–69 274 (6.95) 66 (2.13) 854 (9.05) 1194 (7.25) <0.001 <0.001 <0.001 70–74 240 (6.09) 53 (1.71) 843 (8.93) 1136 (6.89) <0.001 <0.001 <0.001 75–79 203 (5.15) 43 (1.39) 814 (8.62) 1060 (6.43) <0.001 <0.001 <0.001 80–84 175 (4.44) 34 (1.1) 813 (8.61) 1022 (6.2) <0.001 <0.001 <0.001 ≥85 398 (10.1) 63 (2.03) 1675 (17.75) 2136 (12.96) <0.001 <0.001 <0.001 Traveller’s country of origin by income level Low 3 (0.14) 2 (0.1) 7 (0.17) 12 (0.14) 0.776 0.748 0.545 Lower middle 213 (9.59) 127 (6.62) 430 (10.35) 770 (9.29) <0.001 0.338 <0.001 Upper middle 1577 (71.04) 1515 (78.99) 2502 (60.25) 5594 (67.47) <0.001 <0.001 <0.001 High 427 (19.23) 274 (14.29) 1214 (29.23) 1915 (23.1) <0.001 <0.001 <0.001 Open in a new tab Notes: a Ill-defined diseases were excluded. b χ 2 -test was calculated between cause of death groups (C = communicable, I = Injuries, N = = NCDs). NCDs, non-communicable diseases. Reported deaths in travellers aged > 85 years were more often associated with NCDs (17.8 vs 2.0%, P < 0.001) and communicable diseases (10.1 vs 2.0%, P < 0.001) than injuries. Communicable diseases and NCDs exhibited homogenous variation across sexes, though more deaths were recorded among women aged >85 years. While NCDs increased with age, communicable diseases were most often the cause of death in younger age groups. Injury-associated deaths were more often recorded in men (79.9%), with highest burden in the 25–29 years age group ( Table 2 ; Figure 3 ). Country-specific age-sex pyramids by cause of death are shown in supplementary material S9 – S11 (communicable diseases, NCDs, and injuries, respectively). Figure 3. Open in a new tab Number of deaths among international travellers in South America by cause of death ([A] communicable, [B] non-communicable, [C] injuries), age and sex from 2017 to 2021. Notes: Darker colours on the left side of the pyramid indicate males, while lighter colours on the right side of the pyramid indicate females. Regions within countries In Argentina, the highest mortality risk was found in the northern provinces bordering Bolivia [Jujuy (IRR = 51), Salta (IRR = 29)], Paraguay [Formosa (IRR = 62)] and Brazil [Misiones (IRR = 37)]. In Brazil, the highest risks were observed in the North and Central West regions, with Roraima [bordering Venezuela (IRR = 863)] and Mato Grosso do Sul [bordering Paraguay (IRR = 190)] having the highest IRRs. In Chile, the mortality risk was relatively evenly distributed, with the highest risk in Arica, located in the northern region near the border with Peru (IRR = 20). In Colombia, more than a third of deaths were concentrated in the Norte de Santander department, which borders Venezuela (IRR = 160). In Ecuador, the highest risk of mortality was present in Galapagos (IRR = 1194) and in the northern provinces [Sucumbios (IRR = 153) and Carchi (IRR = 130), bordering Colombia]. In Peru, the highest risk of death was observed in Tumbes [bordering Ecuador (IRR = 90)], Tacna [bordering Chile (IRR = 40)] and the capital city of Lima (IRR = 53). In Uruguay, the most substantial risk was observed in the northern [Artigas (IRR = 43), Rivera (IRR = 37)] and eastern [Rocha (IRR = 42)] departments ( Figure 4 ). Country-specific differences in the reported causes of death are presented in supplementary material S12 . The unadjusted distribution of deaths by region within a country are presented in supplementary material S13 . Figure 4. Open in a new tab Geographical distribution of incidence rate ratios by subnational region for all causes of death Among International Travellers in South America (2017–2021). Avoidable and non-avoidable deaths The majority of deaths ( n = 11 770, 68.2%) could have been avoided, including the deaths due to causes that could have been preventable (32.9%), preventable and treatable (19.5%) and treatable (15.8%) ( supplementary material S14 and S15 ). We observed differences across countries; over 85% of deaths could have been avoided in Ecuador (88.8%) and Uruguay (86.3%), while only 60.6% of deaths could have been avoided in Chile. Discussion Our study is the first to compile regional data and provide a comprehensive quantification of the burden and causes of death among international travellers in South America. The findings highlight key heterogeneities and differential risk across and within countries. Nearly half of the deaths were recorded among Venezuelan travellers, reflecting a migratory crisis in South America. The largest death burden, responsible for more than half of the recorded deaths, was associated with NCDs. The COVID-19 pandemic impacted the number of communicable diseases–associated deaths between 2020 and 2021, which surpassed those of NCDs during that period. Most deaths in travellers could have been avoided. Demographic characteristics were associated with specific causes of death; NCDs were responsible for more deaths in older travellers and injury-associated deaths among younger male travellers. NCDs, in particular cardiovascular diseases, were the main causes of death among travellers in smaller studies performed in individual countries. 30–32 Leggat et al. reported on 1000 deaths among travellers to Australia and noted that cardiovascular diseases and cancer were associated with 40% of deaths. 31 Although the distribution of the overall causes of death in our study were similar for cardiovascular diseases and cancer, in the study by Leggat et al. , NCDs caused three out of four deaths. These differences might be explained by the origin of the travellers and differences in the risks profile of the destinations. Nonetheless, our study reflects the emerging global burden of NCDs as the leading cause of death worldwide, mirroring the Global Burden of Disease study estimates that 64.5% of deaths worldwide were attributable to NCDs in 2021. 24 Communicable diseases were the second most common cause of death in travellers to South America. Rather than exotic tropical infections, lower respiratory tract infections caused the highest burden among the communicable diseases. Information on the aetiology of these infections was not consistently available, but the peaks of communicable diseases–related deaths between 2020 and 2021 coincide with times of high SARS-CoV-2 activity in the continent. 33 In Latin America, the pandemic overwhelmed the public health system, where poor infrastructure and lack of effective response led to reports of some of the highest mortality rates worldwide. 34 Peru, in particular, reported one of the highest COVID-19 mortalities per capita in the world, potentially explaining in part the high proportion of deaths among travellers. 34 The incidence and severity of lower respiratory tract infections caused by SARS-CoV-2, S. pneumoniae , respiratory syncytial virus and influenza virus can be reduced by immunization, resulting in lower mortality. These results underscore the critical need to improve pre-travel counselling and preventive services for travellers and immigrants to ensure that age-appropriate vaccinations are recommended. Injury-associated deaths among travellers were the least common in our study. Despite that, they accounted for 18.1% of deaths, which is three times higher than the global estimates for injury-associated deaths among locals. 24 Intentional injuries and motor vehicle–associated deaths were the most common causes of injury-associated deaths and, in some countries, they were more common than communicable diseases and most NCDs. Intentional injuries were the fourth most common cause of death in our study, and violence accounted for the vast majority of those deaths. Colombia and Brazil stood out as the countries with the highest proportion of intentional deaths. South America ranks third out of 22 regions in criminality. 35 Latin Americas and the Caribbean have the highest rate of homicidal deaths in the world, which could be potentially associated with organized crime. 36 Colombia and Brazil have the highest homicide rates in South America, especially in areas with drug trafficking and gang violence. Travel health providers should be aware of the potential areas of risk in South America and recommend that travellers follow international safety guidelines and develop strategies to avoid being a crime victim. 37 , 38 Motor vehicle–associated injuries is a common cause of death among travellers. 39–41 Young male travellers often engage in riskier activities or exhibit risk-taking behaviour (e.g. not wearing seat belts or helmets, riding motorbikes) that results in higher mortality associated with injuries compared to females and older-aged travellers. 30–32 , 42–45 Enhancing pre-travel counselling and developing targeted prevention strategies for young travellers is essential to raise awareness of safety and violence-related injuries. However, strong local regulations and public health measures are also vital for effectively reducing travel-related deaths. In Brazil and Colombia, the highest risk of mortality was observed in remote and sparsely populated regions bordering Venezuela (i.e. Roraima and Norte de Santander), with over 80% of deaths in those regions involving Venezuelan citizens. Although we were not able to distinguish non-migrant from migrant travellers, we believe that migration (i.e. move for permanent or long-term settlement, compared to short-term stays in travellers) routes through remote areas, particularly by displaced populations (e.g. Venezuelans), might be driving these results. Migrants may face adverse environmental conditions, terrain hazards, violent criminals and human traffickers. The influx of displaced migrants and refugees (many crossing borders illegally) also poses significant challenges to the host countries’ government and healthcare systems. 46–49 Across South America, border regions reported the highest mortality, probably coinciding with areas where healthcare systems are less robust and law and order are more difficult to enforce. However, except for Lima in Peru, the mortality risk was lower in capital cities, metropolitan areas and major tourist locations. Limitations Our findings need to be interpreted while considering some limitations stemming from the study design and information sources. Seriously ill travellers may be evacuated from rural to metropolitan regions for specialized medical treatment; thus, the location of death may not always reflect where the travellers became ill or suffered an injury. Furthermore, some travellers may be evacuated back to their home countries. Although we used data from official registries from Ministries of Health, ICD-10 coding for underlying cause of death is known to have issues related to quality and inconsistency within and across countries. 50 , 51 The definition of ‘non-resident’ may differ across countries, introducing some heterogeneity between countries, urging caution to interpret ‘at-risk regions’ within and not between countries. Data from three countries in South America were not accessible, and reporting of the data had limitations in two other countries (Chile and Uruguay). Nonetheless, the seven countries included in the study account for >90% of all officially reported international arrivals in South America. 27 The local population was used as a proxy for travel volume to specific locations to estimate the IRR. The assumption may not always hold, particularly in low population regions with relatively high volume of tourists (e.g. Amazon). In addition, information on the type of traveller (e.g. tourist, visiting friends and relatives, asylum seeker) and travel itinerary (e.g. date of arrival, countries visited) were not available. These factors influence the risk profile of the traveller 52 and can be examined in future studies when more detailed data become available. Furthermore, OECD’s avoidable and non-avoidable deaths classification may have limitations for categorizing diseases with varying severity (e.g. cancers) and might not adequately consider the limited effectiveness of interventions and treatments, quality of care, etc. Nonetheless, it is one of the most comprehensive classifications used internationally. Conclusions Our study unveiled the significant burden and diverse causes of death among international travellers in South America. NCDs were the predominant cause of death, closely followed by deaths from communicable diseases, notably exacerbated by the COVID-19 pandemic. These findings underscore a pressing need for improved pre-travel consultations among other than refugees, emphasizing age-specific vaccinations and safety measures to mitigate risks from both uncontrolled underlying health conditions and injuries. Specifically, the high burden of injury-associated deaths, particularly from motor vehicle accidents and intentional injuries, calls for targeted preventive strategies. However, the effectiveness of measures could vary depending on the targeted populations, as some travellers may lack access to health services. Furthermore, our findings not only reflect the urgent need to enhance public health infrastructure and response in Latin America amid Venezuelan migratory crisis particularly in border regions. Finally, our study highlights the pressing need for ongoing research to refine travel health advice and interventions, aiming to significantly reduce the preventable deaths in travellers. Supplementary Material sup_material_taae119 sup_material_taae119.docx (4.1MB, docx) Contributor Information Kasim Allel, Health Economics Research Centre, Nuffield Department of Population Health, University of Oxford, Old Road Campus, OX3 7LF, Oxfordshire, UK; Department of Infectious Diseases, School of Medicine, Pontificia Universidad Católica, Libertador Bernando O''Higgins 340, Santiago, Chile. Miguel M Cabada, Division of Infectious Diseases, University of Texas Medical Branch, 1000 Strand Street Galveston, Texas 77550, USA; Cusco Branch – Alexander von Humboldt Tropical Medicine Institute, Universidad Peruana Cayetano Heredia, Av Tullumayo 608, Cusco 84, Cusco, Peru. Behzad Kiani, UQ Centre for Clinical Research, Faculty of Medicine, The University of Queensland, Building 71/918 RBWH Herston, Brisbane City QLD 4006, Queensland, Australia. Beatris Mario Martin, UQ Centre for Clinical Research, Faculty of Medicine, The University of Queensland, Building 71/918 RBWH Herston, Brisbane City QLD 4006, Queensland, Australia. Melinda Tanabe, Division of Infectious Diseases, University of Texas Medical Branch, 1000 Strand Street Galveston, Texas 77550, USA. Angela Cadavid Restrepo, School of Public Health, Faculty of Medicine, The University of Queensland, 266 Herston Rd, Herston QLD 4006, Queensland, Australia. Gabriela De Souza Dos Santos, State Health Department of Parana, 828230-140, Curitiba, Brazil. Susana Lloveras, Hospital de Enfermedades Infecciosas F. J. Muñiz, Uspallata 2272, C1282A, Buenos Aires, Argentina. Wondimeneh Shiferaw, UQ Centre for Clinical Research, Faculty of Medicine, The University of Queensland, Building 71/918 RBWH Herston, Brisbane City QLD 4006, Queensland, Australia. Benn Sartorius, UQ Centre for Clinical Research, Faculty of Medicine, The University of Queensland, Building 71/918 RBWH Herston, Brisbane City QLD 4006, Queensland, Australia. Deborah J Mills, UQ Centre for Clinical Research, Faculty of Medicine, The University of Queensland, Building 71/918 RBWH Herston, Brisbane City QLD 4006, Queensland, Australia; Dr Deb The Travel Doctor, Travel Medicine Alliance, 5/247 Adelaide St, Brisbane City QLD 4000, Queensland, Australia. Colleen L Lau, UQ Centre for Clinical Research, Faculty of Medicine, The University of Queensland, Building 71/918 RBWH Herston, Brisbane City QLD 4006, Queensland, Australia. Luis Furuya-Kanamori, UQ Centre for Clinical Research, Faculty of Medicine, The University of Queensland, Building 71/918 RBWH Herston, Brisbane City QLD 4006, Queensland, Australia. Funding C.L.L. was supported by an NHMRC Investigator Grant (APP1193826). L.F.K. was supported by The University of Queensland’s Amplify Initiative and received funding from the Australian Infectious Diseases Research Centre. The funders had no role in the study design, data collection and analysis, decision to publish or preparation of the manuscript. Author contributions Kasim Allel (Conceptualization, Data curation, Formal analysis, Investigation, Methodology, Resources, Software, Validation, Visualization, Writing—original draft, Writing—review & editing [equal]), Miguel Cabada (Conceptualization, Data curation, Investigation, Methodology, Resources, Supervision, Validation, Writing—review & editing [equal]), Behzad Kiani (Data curation, Formal analysis, Methodology, Resources, Software, Writing—review & editing [equal]), Beatris Martin (Resources, Writing—review & editing [equal]), Melinda Tanabe (Data curation, Resources, Writing—review & editing [equal]), Angela Restrepo (Resources, Writing—review & editing [equal]), Gabriela De Souza Dos Santos (Resources, Writing—review & editing [equal]), Susana Lloveras (Resources, Writing—revie