INSERT INTO corpus VALUES(1265,'https://pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/articles/PMC4766644','The Paediatric Ambulatory Consulting Service (PACS) program: a role for family pediatricians in the hospital emergency rooms.','Nigri L, Piazzolla R, Pettoello-Mantovani M, Giardino I, Abbinante M, Gorgoni G.','2016','barletta andria trani rationalize puglia lhc federazione pediatri fimp fimp rpp foggia saturday festivities turnout correspondent rationalize mpm mpm mpm mpm nigri lnigri tiscalinet piazzolla piaroger pettoello pettoellomantovani unifg giardino giardino unifg micaela abbinante abbinante gorgoni gorgonister 17zwbkh pag jill baren rothrock lance blvd pa19103 mathauera wittenbecherb 756a istat istat datasetcode dcis_popres1 23january ministero 23january','Abstract This paper describes the Paediatric Ambulatory Consulting Service (PACS) project, developed by ASL-BT (Azienda Sanitaria Locale, Barletta-Andria-Trani), an Italian regional Public Health Centers network, in response to the current global situation of economic distress. PACS consist in integrating existing public health care services that are independently provided by hospitals and the Primary Care Paediatrics network. It has been developed with the aim to establish innovative yet efficient managerial solutions able to rationalize the resources not weakening the quality of services provided to the population. Keywords: Hospitalization, Economics Correspondence/Findings This paper describes the Paediatric Ambulatory Consulting Service (PACS) project. PACS was developed in 2014 in the Italian Region of Puglia, as a result of a close co-operation between the ASL-BT, a State Local Health Centers network (LHC) [ 1 ], and the Italian Federation of Paediatricians (Federazione Italiana Medici Pediatri, FIMP) in response to the current general situation of economic distress. Both ASL-BT and FIMP are part of the Regional post-graduate medical education network, and collaborate with the Residency Program in Paediatrics (RPP) of the University of Foggia. PACS integrates existing public health care services, otherwise independently provided by public hospitals and Primary Care Paediatric networks. The PACS program, operated by family pediatricians working for the State network of Family Paediatrics, consists in a pediatric ambulatory consulting service, active in hospitals and providing a screening of the clinical conditions of outpatients <18 years old, before they access the Emergency Room (ER) Departments. PACS is active during the week-ends (Saturday-Sunday) and festivities, which in the Italian healthcare system are not covered by the public health services, that are usually provided by the Family Paediatrics only during the weekdays (Monday-Friday). In Italy, like in other nations with similar public health systems, a massive turnout of patients is faced by hospital’s ERs, when the family doctors rest in accordance to their standard work contract agreements. Paediatric ER departments are usually active in Italy only in few large hospitals throughout the Nation, and in the remaining hospitals, pediatricians are generally not included in the ERs teams. Such situation depending mostly upon the need of containing the health care costs. Typically, children are referred by the ER directly to the correspondent hospital Paediatric Units, even for cases showing not serious medical conditions, usually classified as “white” codes [ 2 ], and it often generates an incorrect hospitalization of children. This common situation represents a heavy burden for the hospital administration, due to the high mean daily unit cost of the pediatric hospitalization, usually related to the regional Diagnosis Related Groups (DRG) price list [ 3 ]. Therefore, PACS program provides a first clinical assessment and care for subjects <18 years old whose tutors claim the existence of a pathologic condition when turning out to the hospital ER Departments. The patients classified as “white codes” and “green codes” [ 2 ] are cared in the PACS room service and only the patients classified as “yellow” and “red codes” are forwarded to ER for further assistance, which may include hospitalization depending on the clinical conditions. The rotations of pediatricians in PACS include eight hours working periods, divided in two daily segments. Typically, 9 am to 12 pm, and 4-7 pm. Findings During 2014 the PACS program covered a total of 3.300 h of medical service, involving all the 5 provincial public hospitals managed by the ASL-BT. The total population resident in the BAT province and served by the ASL-BT in 2014 was 392.446 [ 4 ] of which 76.563 (19.5 %) was <18 years of age. The total admissions to the PACS service rooms during a 12 months period has been 8.439, including multiple admission (Fig. 1 ). Fig. 1. Open in a new tab Monthly distribution of accesses to the PACS room services during the year 2014. Monthly distribution of the 8439 accesses (<18 years of age) to the PACS room services in the 5 hospitals of ASL-BT participating to the PACS program during the year 2014 The mean age of patients <18 years old cared for in the PACS rooms was between 3.39 and 4.41, and 80.05 % of them were <6 years old (Fig. 2 ). Figure 3 summarizes the twelve more frequent medical conditions observed in children upon their admission to the hospital PACS rooms. Fig. 2. Open in a new tab Age of subjects <18 years old admitted to the PACS rooms during 2014. Age of subjects <18 years old admitted to the PACS rooms of the 5 ASL-BT hospitals participating to the PACS program during 2014 Fig. 3. Open in a new tab Main signs and symptoms showed by 80 % of the patients admitted to the hospital PACS room services. Main signs and symptoms shown by 80 % (6756) of the 8439 subjects <18 years old admitted to the PACS room services of five hospital participating to the PACS program during the year 2014. The remaining 20 % (1683) presented various different minor symptoms During 2014 the total admissions to the ER departments was reduced by 14 %. In particular, the total cases registered as white and green codes in the ER Departments of the five hospitals included in PACS program were reduced by 54 %, and the number of subjects <18 year of age, hospitalized after their initial admission as white and green codes was significantly reduced by 18 % ( p < 0.001) compared to the previous year (2013). Finally, the PACS program has generated a significant cost containment in hospitalization costs. In fact, considering that the reported mean daily unit cost of hospitalization in Italy [ 5 ] is Euro 612/day, the saving based on a 18 % reduction of admissions generated by the SCAP program has been 110.160,00 euro/1000 hospitalizations/year. Taken together the findings reported suggest PACS to be a positive and cost effective healthcare program. PACS may be considered at national and international level as a useful model for planning future health care programs, aimed at establishing innovative yet efficient managerial solutions able to rationalize the resources not weakening the quality of services provided to the population Footnotes Competing interests The authors declare that they have no competing interests. Authors’ contributions LN, MPM, RP, IG, GG have made substantial contributions to conception and design, or acquisition of data; LN, MPM, RP, IG, MA, GG have made substantial contributions to analysis and interpretation of data; LN, MPM, RP, IG, MA, GG have been involved in drafting the manuscript or revising it critically for important intellectual content and gave their final approval of the version to be published; LN, MPM, RP, IG, MA, GG agree to be accountable for all aspects of the work in ensuring that questions related to the accuracy and integrity of the work are appropriately investigated and resolved. All authors read and approved the final manuscript. Contributor Information Luigi Nigri, Email: lnigri@tiscalinet.it. Ruggiero Piazzolla, Email: piaroger@tin.it. Massimo Pettoello-Mantovani, Phone: 0039. 0881.732488, Email: massimo.pettoellomantovani@unifg.it. Ida Giardino, Email: ida.giardino@unifg.it. Micaela Abbinante, Email: m.abbinante@gmail.com. Giovanni Gorgoni, Email: gorgonister@gmail.com. References 1. State and Territorial health Departments. The US government’s official web portal. http:/1.usa.gov/17zwbKH (accessed 23 January, 2015). 2. Mace SE, Mayer TA. Triage In: Pediatric Emergency Medicine. 2008; Chapter 155, pag. 1087-1096. Edited by:Jill M.Baren, Steven G.Rothrock, John A.Brennan, and Lance Brown. ISBN:978-1-4160-0087-7. Saunders Elsevier, 1600 John F.Kennedy Blvd., Ste 1800, Philadelphia, PA19103-2899. 3. Mathauera I, Wittenbecherb F. Hospital payment systems based on diagnosis-related groups: experiences in low- and middle-income countries. Bull World Health Organ. 2013;91:746–756A. doi: 10.2471/BLT.12.115931. [ DOI ] [ PMC free article ] [ PubMed ] [ Google Scholar ] 4. IStat.Italian census. http://dati.istat.it/Index.aspx?DataSetCode=DCIS_POPRES1 . (accessed 23January, 2015). 5. Ministero della Salute. Italy. www.salute.gov.it (accessed 23January, 2015).',89,'2025-10-10 07:52:13','2025-10-10 07:52:13');
INSERT INTO corpus VALUES(1266,'https://pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/articles/PMC1717596','A countrywide programme of continuing professional development in Argentina. Argentine Society of Paediatrics, Subcommittee of Continuing Paediatric Education.','Lejarraga H, Ageitos ML, Galli A, Castro C.','1998','argentinean educationalists 12topics haggerty lenton dison santer michaelsen erkonen woodhead gilmer turnberg','Abstract The Argentinean Society of Paediatrics introduced in 1993 a continuing professional development (CPD) programme to raise standards of clinical practice. The aims of the project were to introduce a structured, distance learning programme accessible to all paediatricians in the country, but especially for those working far from centres of paediatric excellence. The programme is planned on an annual basis. It includes four activities: a written manual designed by a team of medical experts and educationalists comprising 12topics; field work for participants; annual meetings in several locations in the country for discussion of the subjects; and an evaluation based on centrally designed multiple choice questions distributed by mail. In spite of a registration fee of £90 a year, participation in the programme increased from 3357 in 1993 to 4126 in 1996, from a membership of 10 216 paediatricians in Argentina. The popularity of the programme may result from an appropriate interpretation of professional needs of paediatricians in Argentina, adequate organisational arrangements that reach all colleagues, including those working in remote areas, and a genuine motivation of paediatricians for participating in a learning process. Full Text The Full Text of this article is available as a PDF (83.4 KB). Selected References These references are in PubMed. This may not be the complete list of references from this article. Alpert J. J. Primary care: the future for pediatric education. Pediatrics. 1990 Nov;86(5):653–659. [ PubMed ] [ Google Scholar ] Haggerty R. J. Child health 2000: new pediatrics in the changing environment of children''s needs in the 21st century. Pediatrics. 1995 Oct;96(4 Pt 2):804–812. [ PubMed ] [ Google Scholar ] Lenton S. W., Dison P. J., Haines L. C. A BPA survey of recently appointed consultants. Arch Dis Child. 1994 Oct;71(4):381–385. doi: 10.1136/adc.71.4.381. [ DOI ] [ PMC free article ] [ PubMed ] [ Google Scholar ] Rosenberg W., Donald A. Evidence based medicine: an approach to clinical problem-solving. BMJ. 1995 Apr 29;310(6987):1122–1126. doi: 10.1136/bmj.310.6987.1122. [ DOI ] [ PMC free article ] [ PubMed ] [ Google Scholar ] Santer D. M., Michaelsen V. E., Erkonen W. E., Winter R. J., Woodhead J. C., Gilmer J. S., D''Alessandro M. P., Galvin J. R. A comparison of educational interventions. Multimedia textbook, standard lecture, and printed textbook. Arch Pediatr Adolesc Med. 1995 Mar;149(3):297–302. doi: 10.1001/archpedi.1995.02170150077014. [ DOI ] [ PubMed ] [ Google Scholar ] Turnberg L. Continuing medical education in the NHS. Br J Hosp Med. 1995 Jun 7;53(11):591–592. [ PubMed ] [ Google Scholar ] Walker D. A., Stephenson T., Blair M. Child health education for the year 2000. Arch Dis Child. 1995 Sep;73(3):261–263. doi: 10.1136/adc.73.3.261. [ DOI ] [ PMC free article ] [ PubMed ] [ Google Scholar ]',4,'2025-10-10 07:52:26','2025-10-10 07:52:26');
INSERT INTO corpus VALUES(1267,'https://pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/articles/PMC10008200','Potential use of telemedicine in paediatrics: a single-centre retrospective review.','Sarkari ZZ, Fish C.','2023','otoscope bmjpo 001819supp001 perfecting caters bmjpo bmjpo 642kb maryam taheri pine reken juli anamara ritt zzs brav monaghesh hajizadeh keesara nejmp2005835 chunara ocaa072 e293 badawy e22696 2f22696 etm bmjpo 001819supp001 bmjpo bmjpo 642kb','Abstract Recent advancements and adoption of telemedicine have affected all aspects of healthcare including paediatrics. While telemedicine has the potential to increase access to paediatric care, the limitations of this service in its current iteration bring into question its usefulness for direct replacement of in-person care, particularly in an acute or urgent care setting. This retrospective review demonstrates that only a small percentage of in-person visits to our practice would have resulted in definitive diagnosis and treatment if facilitated via telemedicine. There is a need for better and more widespread data collection techniques and tools suitable for paediatric remote care implementation before telemedicine becomes a useful diagnostic and treatment tool in an acute or urgent care setting. Keywords: Data Collection, Technology Introduction During the COVID-19 pandemic, telemedicine saw over a 600% increase in usage as the healthcare industry transitioned away from in-person care. 1–3 Outside of a pandemic setting, telemedicine, particularly in the paediatric space, also has the potential to provide broader reach and access in areas without these resources. 4 Additionally, telemedicine has shown equal efficacy compared with in-person visits in certain cases as demonstrated by a recent systematic review. 5 It is important to note, however, that this systematic review examined conditions that do not rely heavily on objective data collection such as mental health evaluations or chronic condition management where an in-person visit would have been a part of the management plan. For the acute conditions included in the analysis, data collection tools such as cameras or cellular device otoscope attachment were part of the study design. Telemedicine also comes with several drawbacks, not least of which is that healthcare systems are largely based on in-person interactions. 2 Additionally, quality telemedicine encounters are based on access to costly and non-widely distributed technologies, which tends to favour those with privilege. 6 Our hypothesis is that telemedicine is likely ineffective in terms of definitive diagnosis and treatment in the acute and urgent care setting and that in the absence of data collection tools, most of the visits presenting to our clinic could not be definitively managed via telemedicine alone. In order to evaluate the effectiveness of telemedicine in the acute and urgent care setting, we retrospectively reviewed 2019 visits to our paediatric primary and urgent care clinic in Portland, Oregon, pre the SARS-COV-2 pandemic from 2019 to early 2020. Our clinic provides routine and urgent (non-emergent) care to paediatric patients aged 0–21 years. As the majority of our practice is acute care, telemedicine at our practice is almost entirely used to address new acute concerns or injuries. Methods A retrospective chart review was conducted for all in-person visits at our paediatric primary and urgent care office located in Portland, Oregon. The current study includes 4 months leading up to the start of the COVID-19 pandemic (November 2019 to February 2020). Each patient visit (n=2019) was first categorised into groups deemed automatically incompatible with telemedicine such as need for a procedure or additional workup. Due to the hours of operation and lack of a pre-existing relationship with our patients, sending them to an outside centre for blood work or imaging is not practical. The remainder of the visits were reviewed by an experienced paediatric clinician and divided, based on the available documentation in the note, into whether definitive treatment via telemedicine was likely of value, potentially of value, or not of value. Information on data validation can be found in online supplemental file 1 . Supplementary data bmjpo-2022-001819supp001.pdf (39.9KB, pdf) Results Our study included 1567 distinct patients representing 2019 visits. The majority of patients (n=63.15%) were under 5 years of age. Telemedicine would not have led to definitive diagnosis and treatment for 1350 visits (n=66.86%). Telemedicine would have been potentially useful for definitive diagnosis and treatment for 578 visits (n=28.62%) and would likely have been useful for definitive diagnosis and treatment in 91 visits (n=4.51%). The diagnoses most likely to have been definitively treated with telemedicine are rashes and head/eye/ear/nose/throat/mouth concerns ( table 1 ). Table 1. Visit counts by category grouped by likelihood that telemedicine would lead to definitive diagnosis and treatment Telemedicine likely of value Telemedicine potentially of value Telemedicine not of value Total Cold/respiratory symptoms 3 172 236 411 Head/eye/ear/nose/throat/mouth concern 43 156 66 265 Fever 2 91 40 133 Gastrointestinal/genitourinary concern 0 56 53 109 Rash 38 54 30 122 Injury 3 25 76 104 Behaviour change 0 18 9 27 Procedure 0 3 232 235 Other 2 3 22 27 Additional workup needed 0 0 586 586 Total 91 578 1350 2019 Open in a new tab Discussion Our findings support our hypothesis that the majority of paediatric patients who visited our practice during the study period could not have been treated definitively via telemedicine. Rather, telemedicine is of most utility as an augmented triage tool. Given the recent surge in telemedicine services and utilization around the COVID-19 pandemic, it is clear that a significant portion of the proliferation energy in telemedicine solutions could be better used perfecting remote data gathering tools geared specifically towards paediatric patients. We would expect that a substantially higher portion of our visits could have been definitively diagnosed and treated with telemedicine if some basic exam and laboratory data could be obtained at home. There are several limitations of our study. Selection bias by retrospectively analysing in-person visits is one such limitation. In addition, despite analysis by expert paediatric clinicians, there is some subjectivity to judging whether a patient might or might not have been definitively diagnosed and treated via telemedicine. Another limitation is that our clinic almost entirely caters to acute and urgent illnesses and concerns. It is likely that due to the nature of the presenting complaints, telemedicine alone would not be sufficient for diagnosis and treatment without additional patient data. Finally, due to its retrospective nature, our study cannot comment on whether the patients who were deemed to need in-person treatment would have been fine without a visit and vice versa. Supplementary Material Reviewer comments bmjpo-2022-001819.reviewer_comments.pdf (283.2KB, pdf) Author''s manuscript bmjpo-2022-001819.draft_revisions.pdf (642KB, pdf) Acknowledgments The authors would like to thank Maryam Taheri for her support in gathering the research team, Ash Pine and Jason van Reken for their contributions to the data collection and analysis, Juli Moore of Oregon State University for advisory support, and Anamara Ritt of the University of Southern California for advisory support. Footnotes Contributors: ZZS was critical in the planning of the study, data collection and analysis as well as manuscript preparation and revision. CF additionally finalised the study design, collected and analysed data and was responsible for the final manuscript preparation and submission. Funding: The authors have not declared a specific grant for this research from any funding agency in the public, commercial or not-for-profit sectors. Competing interests: None declared. Provenance and peer review: Not commissioned; externally peer reviewed. Supplemental material: This content has been supplied by the author(s). It has not been vetted by BMJ Publishing Group Limited (BMJ) and may not have been peer-reviewed. Any opinions or recommendations discussed are solely those of the author(s) and are not endorsed by BMJ. BMJ disclaims all liability and responsibility arising from any reliance placed on the content. Where the content includes any translated material, BMJ does not warrant the accuracy and reliability of the translations (including but not limited to local regulations, clinical guidelines, terminology, drug names and drug dosages), and is not responsible for any error and/or omissions arising from translation and adaptation or otherwise. Ethics statements Patient consent for publication Not applicable. Ethics approval This study involves human participants and was approved by Pearl IRB, IRB Protocol #20-BRAV-101. References 1. Monaghesh E, Hajizadeh A. The role of telehealth during COVID-19 outbreak: a systematic review based on current evidence. BMC Public Health 2020;20. 10.1186/s12889-020-09301-4 Available: 10.1186/s12889-020-09301-4 [ DOI ] [ PMC free article ] [ PubMed ] [ Google Scholar ] 2. Keesara S, Jonas A, Schulman K. Covid-19 and health care’s digital revolution. N Engl J Med 2020;382:1–3. 10.1056/NEJMp2005835 [ DOI ] [ PubMed ] [ Google Scholar ] 3. Mann DM, Chen J, Chunara R, et al. COVID-19 transforms health care through telemedicine: evidence from the field. J Am Med Inform Assoc 2020;27:1132–5. 10.1093/jamia/ocaa072 [ DOI ] [ PMC free article ] [ PubMed ] [ Google Scholar ] 4. Burke BL, Hall RW, Section on Telehealth Care . Telemedicine: pediatric applications. Pediatrics 2015;136:e293–308. 10.1542/peds.2015-1517 [ DOI ] [ PMC free article ] [ PubMed ] [ Google Scholar ] 5. Shah AC, Badawy SM. Telemedicine in pediatrics: systematic review of randomized controlled trials. JMIR Pediatr Parent 2021;4:e22696. 10.2196/22696 Available: https://doi.org/10.2196%2F22696 [ DOI ] [ PMC free article ] [ PubMed ] [ Google Scholar ] 6. Zhang X-Y, Zhang P. Telemedicine in clinical setting. Exp Ther Med 2016;12:2405–7. 10.3892/etm.2016.3656 [ DOI ] [ PMC free article ] [ PubMed ] [ Google Scholar ] Associated Data This section collects any data citations, data availability statements, or supplementary materials included in this article. Supplementary Materials Supplementary data bmjpo-2022-001819supp001.pdf (39.9KB, pdf) Reviewer comments bmjpo-2022-001819.reviewer_comments.pdf (283.2KB, pdf) Author''s manuscript bmjpo-2022-001819.draft_revisions.pdf (642KB, pdf)',614,'2025-10-10 07:52:40','2025-10-10 07:52:40');
INSERT INTO corpus VALUES(1268,'https://pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/articles/PMC1029367','A career in paediatrics? A survey of paediatric senior house officers in England and Wales.','Barker DP, Buss PW.','1993','shos busiest foreseen macfaul','Abstract A telephone questionnaire was targeted at 189 paediatric senior house officers (SHOs) throughout England and Wales in order to evaluate their attitudes towards paediatrics and gain insight into methods by which recruitment could be improved in future years. A total of 152 senior house officers were interviewed. The group consisted mainly of general practice trainees, but included 51 career paediatricians. The majority were female (57%) and 44% were married. Our assessment revealed a perceived high work-load, but also showed that paediatrics remains a satisfying specialty. Training while in post was perceived as unsatisfactory by 32% of those questioned. Sixty per cent reported a decline in their social life since starting their post. Paediatrics was seen as the busiest of a number of specialties. This perception persisted, and in fact increased, between SHO and consultant grade. It is foreseen that this may lead to further problems in recruitment. Full text PDF Previous 752 753 Next Selected References These references are in PubMed. This may not be the complete list of references from this article. MacFaul R. Paediatric medical manpower. Arch Dis Child. 1993 Jan;68(1):70–72. doi: 10.1136/adc.68.1.70. [ DOI ] [ PMC free article ] [ PubMed ] [ Google Scholar ]',2,'2025-10-10 07:52:50','2025-10-10 07:52:50');
INSERT INTO corpus VALUES(1269,'https://pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/articles/PMC7684064','[Delivery of care pressure, frequency of attendance and pediatric morbidity at a health center. Age and seasonal variations].','Durá Travé T, Gúrpide Ayarra N.','2001','contribuir demanda permita plantear oportunidades mejora diseno retrospectivo observacional emplazamiento estella navarra totalidad atendidos pediatricas edades dividieron lactantes preescolares escolares adolescentes mediciones atendidas registraron fecha sexo demanda programada cipsap calcularon frecuentacion presion distribucion estacional demanda programadas frecuentacion demanda preescolares programadas lactantes presion verano demanda prevalentes respiratorias infecciosas neurosensoriales accidentes digestivas existia correlacion negativa respiratorias mientras directamente proporcional accidentes aparato respiratorias infecciosas otono invierno sometida sobrecarga agudas aparato respiratorio motivo frecuente infecciosas examenes adquiriendo progresivamente relevancia convendria coordinacion unificar diagnosticos terapeuticos cuanto rigurosa extrahospitalaria repercusion mejora infantil morbilidad presion estella navarra feeders cipsap feeders locomotive unify bibliografia conferencia ginebra num leandro cebrian muruzabal ciriza nino conocimientos actitudes satisfaccion usuarios ninos basica cinco despues aten ejido sordo efectividad nino asmatico desarrollado aten mauleon gurpide infantil adolescente examenes periodicos medifam penalver puente andalucia seccion extrahospitalaria contenido extrahospitalaria morbilidad frecuentacion repeticionen gimenez ogando rincon riquelme nino pediatra asensi grafia demanda futuro extrahospitalaria aspecto supl gude morbilidad candela quirce mancheno demanda alicante aten illana ichaso narvaiz asociados presion valls carballo demanda pediatricas extrahospitalarias vigo salom marmaneu paricio indicadores rendimiento supl salom indicadores morbilidad comunidad valenciana bol sansa orus juncosa barredo frecuentacion hospitalarios utilizacion bellon niveles ansiedad materna utilizacion newachek paricio salom diagnosticos extrahospitalaria experiencia informatizada','Abstract Objetivo Contribuir a un mayor conocimiento de la demanda asistencial pediátrica en atención primaria que permita plantear oportunidades de mejora en la calidad asistencial. Diseño Estudio retrospectivo, observacional. Emplazamiento Centro de Salud de Estella (Navarra). Pacientes La totalidad de pacientes atendidos en el año 1999 en una de las 2 consultas pediátricas del centro de salud. Por edades, se dividieron en lactantes (0-12 meses), preescolares (1-5 años), escolares (6-9 años) y adolescentes (10-14 años). Mediciones De las 6.611 consultas atendidas se registraron fecha, sexo, edad, tipo de consulta (demanda/programada) y problema de salud (CIPSAP-2), y se calcularon la frecuentación y presión asistencial en relación con la edad y tipo de consulta, así como la distribución estacional. Resultados Las consultas a demanda fueron 4.600 (69,6%) y las programadas 2.011 (30,4%). La frecuentación total fue de 5,46, en las consultas a demanda fue mayor en los preescolares (6,4) y en las programadas en los lactantes (13,3). La presión asistencial total fue de 28,2, siendo menor (p < 0,05) en los meses de verano. En las consultas a demanda los problemas de salud más prevalentes fueron: enfermedades respiratorias (52,8%), infecciosas (7,5%), neurosensoriales (6,8%), accidentes (6,0%) y digestivas (4,7%). Existía una correlación negativa (p < 0,05) entre la edad y la prevalencia de las enfermedades respiratorias, mientras que la edad era directamente proporcional (p < 0,05) a la prevalencia de accidentes y enfermedades del aparato locomotor. La prevalencia de las enfermedades respiratorias e infecciosas era mayor (p < 0,05) en los meses de otoño e invierno. Conclusiones La pediatría en un centro de salud está sometida a una sobrecarga asistencial, siendo las enfermedades agudas del aparato respiratorio el motivo de consulta más frecuente y, en menor grado, las enfermedades infecciosas. Los exámenes de salud están adquiriendo progresivamente una mayor relevancia. Convendría mayor coordinación entre los EAP para unificar criterios diagnósticos y terapéuticos, ya que cuanto más rigurosa sea la pediatría extrahospitalaria mayor será su repercusión sobre la mejora de la calidad de la salud infantil. Palabras clave: Atención primaria, Morbilidad, Pediatría, Presión asistencial Abstract Objective To inform on the demand for primary care paediatric services, with a view to improving health care delivery. Design Observational retrospective study. Setting Estella Health Centre (Navarra). Patients All patients attended during 1999 at one of the two paediatric clinics at the health centre. They were divided by ages into breast-feeders (0-12 months), pre-school (1-5 years old), primary school (6-9) and adolescents (10-14). Measurements The date, sex, age, type of consultation (on demand/scheduled) and health problem (CIPSAP-2) of the 6611 consultations were recorded. Frequency of visits, care pressure and seasonal distribution, related to age and type of consultation, were calculated. Results There were 4600 on-demand consultations (69.6%) and 2011 scheduled ones (30.4%). Total frequency of attendance was 5.46, which was greater in on-demand pre-school children (6.4) and scheduled breast-feeders (13.3). Overall patient pressure was 28.2, though less (p < 0.05) in the summer months. The most common health problems in on-demand consultations were respiratory illnesses (52.8%), infectious diseases (7.5%), neuro-sensory problems (6.8%), accidents (6.0%) and digestive problems (4.7%). There was negative correlation (p < 0.05) between age and the prevalence of respiratory diseases, whereas age was directly proportional (p < 0.05) to the prevalence of accidents and locomotive illnesses. Respiratory and infectious diseases were more common (p < 0.05) in the autumn and winter months. Conclusions Paediatrics at a health centre suffers patient overload, with acute illnesses of the respiratory apparatus, and to a lesser extent infectious diseases, the main causes of consultation. Health check-ups are becoming steadily more important. There needs to be better coordination between PC teams in order to unify diagnostic and therapeutic criteria, for the more thorough non-hospital paediatrics becomes, the greater its effect on improving child health quality. Key words: Primary care, Morbidity, Paediatrics, Care pressure Bibliografía 1. Conferencia Internacional sobre Atención primaria . OMS-UNICEF; Ginebra: 1978. Alma-Ata, 1978. [ Google Scholar ] 2. Ley 14/1986, de 25 de abril, General de Sanidad. BOE núm. 102 (29/4/1986). 3. Leandro S.V., Cebrián Y., Hernández M., Muruzábal M.C., Moreno V., Ciriza M.V. Programa de control del niño sano: evaluación de conocimientos, actitudes y satisfacción de los usuarios. Acta Ped Esp. 1993;51:95–98. [ Google Scholar ] 4. Padilla F.M. Prevalencia de caries dental en niños en una zona básica de salud. Cinco años después. Aten Primaria. 1998;21:540–544. [ PubMed ] [ Google Scholar ] 5. Díaz C.A., Alonso L.M., García M.T., Del Ejido J., Sordo M.A., Alonso J.C. Evaluación de las actividades y de la efectividad de un programa del niño asmático desarrollado en atención primaria. Aten Primaria. 1997;19:199–206. [ PubMed ] [ Google Scholar ] 6. Durá T., Mauleón C., Gúrpide N. Programa de Atención a la Población Infantil y Adolescente: cumplimiento de los exámenes periódicos de salud. MEDIFAM. 1999;9:367–371. [ Google Scholar ] 7. Hernández M.D., Peñalver I., Puente E. Situación actual de la pediatría de atención primaria en Andalucía. Revista Pediatría Atención Primaria. 1999;1:31–35. [ Google Scholar ] 8. Sección de Pediatría Extrahospitalaria de la Asociación Española de Pediatría. Estudio del contenido de las consultas en pediatría extrahospitalaria. An Esp Pediatr. 1990;32:241–245. [ PubMed ] [ Google Scholar ] 9. Manuel M.J., Moreno A. Morbilidad, frecuentación e índice de repeticiónen una consulta de pediatría. Acta Ped Esp. 1994;52:479–482. [ Google Scholar ] 10. Giménez A.M., Ogando B., García L.M., Rincón P., Riquelme M. Atención al niño en un centro de salud ¿Pediatra o médico general? Acta Ped Esp. 1995;53:31–36. [ Google Scholar ] 11. García L.A., Asensi A., Grafiá C., Coll P. Estudio de la demanda en atención primaria pediátrica. An Esp Pediatr. 1996;44:469–474. [ PubMed ] [ Google Scholar ] 12. Marina C. Futuro de la pediatría extrahospitalaria. Aspecto asistencial. An Esp Pediatr. 1991;35(Supl 45):23–25. [ PubMed ] [ Google Scholar ] 13. Pérez I., Gallego D., Gude F. Morbilidad pediátrica en un centro de salud. An Esp Pediatr. 1991;4:15–18. [ PubMed ] [ Google Scholar ] 14. Arenas S., Candela C., Quirce F., Marco D., Mancheño J., Tobías J. Evaluación de la demanda asistencial en un centro de atención primaria en Alicante. Aten Primaria. 1990;7:468. [ PubMed ] [ Google Scholar ] 15. Rodríguez B., López A., Illana F., Ichaso M.S., Pérez N., Narváiz J. Análisis de los factores asociados a la presión asistencial en un área de atención primaria. Centro de Salud. 1994;2:795–800. [ Google Scholar ] 16. Valls T., Carballo C., Martínez R., Martínez P., Espinosa M. Estudio de la demanda en las consultas pediátricas extrahospitalarias de Vigo. An Esp Pediatr. 1989;30:45–50. [ PubMed ] [ Google Scholar ] 17. Salom A., Marmaneu M.D., Paricio J.M. Indicadores de rendimiento de consulta pediátrica. An Esp Pediatr. 1990;33(Supl 41):99–100. [ Google Scholar ] 18. Salom A. Indicadores de morbilidad en atención primaria en la Comunidad Valenciana. Bol Soc Val Pediatr. 1991;14:15–18. [ Google Scholar ] 19. Sansa L.l., Orús T., Juncosa S., Barredo M. Frecuentación a los servicios de urgencias hospitalarios: relación con la utilización de la atención primaria en una población pediátrica. An Esp Pediatr. 1994;44:105–108. [ PubMed ] [ Google Scholar ] 20. Sánchez F.J., Bellón J.A., Castillo R. Niveles de ansiedad materna y utilización pediátrica en atención primaria. An Esp Pediatr. 1993;38:423–427. [ PubMed ] [ Google Scholar ] 21. Newachek P.W. Characteristics of children with high and low usage of physician services. Med Care. 1992;30:30–32. doi: 10.1097/00005650-199201000-00003. [ DOI ] [ PubMed ] [ Google Scholar ] 22. Paricio J.M., Salom A., De Castro P. Diagnósticos en pediatría extrahospitalaria. Una experiencia informatizada de 6 años. An Esp Pediatr. 1991;34:451–453. [ PubMed ] [ Google Scholar ]',2,'2025-10-10 07:53:02','2025-10-10 07:53:02');
INSERT INTO corpus VALUES(1270,'https://pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/articles/PMC11042264','Standardisation of intravenous infusion therapy for paediatrics: big challenges for the little patients.','Taladriz-Sender I, Manrique-Rodríguez S, Fernández-Llamazares CM, Herranz-Alonso A, Sanjurjo-Sáez M.','2024','standardising jeopardises jeopardises standardising homogenise ismp standardising ismp ismp vesicant consensuses farmacia hospitalaria omeprazole intermittently vesicant acyclovir adrenalin bitartrate ampoule alprostadil ampoule alteplase ampoule trihydrate clavulanate aztreonam ampoule ampoule ampoule caspofungin pentahydrate pentahydrate avibactam ceftolozane ejhpharm 003316supp001 intermittently diluents diluents alteplase trihydrate clavulanate caspofungin ertapenem procainamide epoprostenol flecainide standardising standardising variabilities intermittently intermittently resorted championed barcode maximises reachable homogenising ceciliamfll aherranza amelia caridad cordero belen arriaga redondo llorente sonia tania carbayo rivas regina abujeta soria postigo gordillo yunquera muro brussi sergio jaquete belen marrodan leira castelos soledad regueira yolanda cristina susana clemente bautista francisca munar bauza cabanas poy cesar campillo quesada aida felipe villalobos montserrat fontanals caravaca palou cristina tania goretti susana enrique lorena busto parada collado caparros joaquin jurado bellon javier beatriz corro jon igartua araudogoitia larburu aristicabal valeron lemaur dolores raquel salmeron fagundo montanana sonia garces carcel noguera borras alejandra robles montserrat margarita cuervas mons vendrell elia valderrabanos javier portero prados antonia vayo manrique cecilia llamazares herranz sanjurjo cmf cmf cmf cmf aranaz aibar remon vitaller burillo preventability eneas mzp047 ghaleb 1g717 stockwell toomey e20173360 ick ghaleb corro llamazares marrodan multicentrico evitabilidad incidentes medicamentos acuden pediatricas canobbio ejhpharm cotrina luque aznar vayo benito lista medicamentos anpedi jointcommission tjc ismp errores medicacion ismp ficheros 28septiembre manrique llamazares estandarizar reto asumible hilmas sowan gaffoor ajhp080598 manrique heras pernia s40268 oskarsdottir jphp medicamento listado medicamentos ismp ficheros medicamentos 20alto 20riesgo noncytotoxic vesicant learningcenter ins1 noncytotoxic vesicant mist mist mist micromedexsolutions cavell palliativedrugs buildingasafernhsforpatients vtoxford standardising standardising taketomo mcleroy alzate e18502 s4s ashx tunstell jphp manrique llamazares artmed aguado jphp ejhpharm 003316supp001','Abstract Background Worldwide organisations advocate standardising the management of intravenous drugs as an essential strategy to increase safety in paediatric healthcare settings. Intravenous administration is a route associated with some potential complications. Many adverse events are related to the use of intravenous medications, and the great variability in their handling and preparation represents an added risk that jeopardises the safety of children. Purpose To standardise the dilutions of intravenous drugs most commonly administered to Spanish hospitalised paediatric and neonatal patients. Methods The process leading to the standardisation of concentrations was undertaken following a two-round modified Delphi procedure. The consensus included the most common drugs administered by continuous or intermittent intravenous infusion to hospitalised and/or critically ill paediatric patients. Results For paediatric patients, the proposal included a total of 102 drugs (45 continuous infusion and 59 intermittent infusion), with 192 concentrations to be standardised. The final consensus included 101 drugs (99%), of which 44 were continuous infusion and 59 intermittent infusion; 160 concentrations were standardised (72.7%). For neonates, the initial proposal included 80 drugs (38 continuous infusion and 43 intermittent infusion), with 189 concentrations to be standardised. The final consensus included 80 drugs (100%), of which 38 were continuous infusion and 43 were intermittent infusion; 120 concentrations were standardised (49.2%). Conclusions This proposal showed that standardisation is a feasible approach that can be reached by other healthcare institutions. It can be used in other centres and contribute in the future to unifying paediatric clinical practice. Keywords: PEDIATRICS; NEONATOLOGY; Administration, Intravenous; Safety; PHARMACEUTICAL PREPARATIONS What is already known on this topic Intravenous drug administration plays an important role in hospitalised paediatric patients. Many adverse events are related to the use of intravenous medications and the great variability in their handling and preparation represents a risk that jeopardises the safety of children. Worldwide organisations advocate standardising the management of intravenous drugs as an essential strategy to increase safety in paediatric healthcare settings. What this study adds This work has shown that standardisation is a feasible approach that can be exported to other organisations. How this study might affect research, practice or policy This project might lay the foundations for the development of national recommendations and it could homogenise criteria for the safe management of intravenous drugs in paediatric clinical practice. Introduction Patient safety is such an essential component of quality in the clinical setting that the World Health Organization considers it a priority of healthcare. 1 A study on the adverse events resulting from hospitalisation carried out in Spain in 2005 found that 37.4% were medication-related, with 34.8% being avoidable. 2 Several studies have shown that the incidence and potential severity of medication errors (MEs) in paediatric and neonatal populations are greater than in adults. 3 4 This is due to a number of causes, including the different drug pharmacokinetic and pharmacodynamic characteristics compared with adults and the fact that the formulation and packaging of medications are prepared for adults. In addition, children and neonates are often cared for in facilities tailored to adults and they are also commonly unable to express their concern or tolerance or to alert caregivers about MEs. 5 6 Although MEs may occur at any stage of the drug utilisation process, prescription and administration phases have the highest incidence, 7 particularly in the case of intravenous medications. It is known that 60% of life-threatening adverse events are related to intravenous high-risk drugs and/or medicines with a narrow therapeutic margin. 8 For this reason, ensuring patient safety when managing intravenous therapy should be a priority for any healthcare organisation. Variability in the handling and preparation of intravenous medications is one of the most common and significant challenges in paediatric clinical practice. Standardisation of infusion therapy has been shown to reduce inaccuracies in preparation and enhances efficiency of pharmacies. 9 In this regard, organisations such as the Institute for Safe Medication Practices (ISMP) 10 and the Joint Commission on Accreditation of Healthcare Organizations recommend standardising high-risk medicines as a safe practice 911 capable of reducing and preventing MEs both in paediatric 12 13 and adult patients. Despite this, a large-scale survey has shown the variation within healthcare systems regarding standardisation of infusion preparations. 14 The aim of the present project was to standardise concentrations of intravenous medications that should be administered to hospitalised paediatric and neonate patients. Materials and methods Establishing expert advisory panels Two multidisciplinary expert advisory panels were created for the standardisation process. These boards comprised healthcare providers with experience in paediatric and/or neonate clinical care, as well as in vascular access management and drug safety. The pharmacist participants were members of the Group of Paediatric Pharmacy of the Spanish Association of Hospital Pharmacy. Each pharmacist proposed the candidates from their hospital who were renowned in the field of paediatric and neonatal care in Spain. To ensure the independence of the standardisation processes in both groups, most members participated only in one of the two panels, as a function of their past experience. The concentrations agreed by consensus had to be compatible with all the clinical scenarios that could potentially arise in both the paediatric and the neonate context. The paediatric panel consisted of 43 healthcare providers from 15 Spanish hospitals: 13 intensivists, 6 anesthesiologists, 11 nurses and 13 hospital pharmacists. The neonatology panel consisted of 34 healthcare providers from 13 Spanish hospitals: 9 neonatologists, 13 nurses and 12 hospital pharmacists. Development of standard medication concentrations During the first stage three paediatric and neonate-trained pharmacists determined an initial list of medicines to standardise their concentrations. The chosen medications included the most common drugs administered intravenously through continuous or intermittent infusion to hospitalised and/or critically ill paediatric or neonate patients according to centre-implemented protocols and consumption reports. The proposed list was shared and enlarged following consultations with the other pharmacists on the panel. Intravenous boluses and specialty drugs (eg, oncology, radiology) were excluded. The final list highlighted all the drugs considered high risk on the ISMP List of High Alert Medications. 15 Additionally, in agreement with the proposal published by other authors, some paediatric-focused high-risk drugs with either a narrow therapeutic margin or a higher incidence of MEs in the paediatric population were also included. 8 The combination of ISMP and literature guidelines was crucial to developing a comprehensive list of high-risk drugs for the standardisation process in a paediatric and neonate context. Each drug was also classified according to its vesicant nature as a high-risk feature involving the administration process. 16 Once the final list was obtained, the initial three paediatric and neonate-trained pharmacists proposed one or several potential concentrations for each of the drugs included. Although there were no limits to the number of concentrations per drug, final consensus agreed as limited a number as possible to be flexible enough for a wide range of patient weights and to decrease MEs due to high variability in management. Determination of the concentrations considered recommended concentrations published in the literature and in the protocols of Spanish and foreign hospitals, experience gained from routine clinical practice, and concentrations recommended by manufacturers. When available, the ready-to-use pre-diluted industrial product must be prioritised. Standardisation of intravenous therapy The standardisation phase was carried out using a two-round modified Delphi method and involved all the expert panel members. The paediatrics and neonatology consensuses were agreed on simultaneously and independently. The first Delphi round was conducted between June and August 2020. The list of drugs and their proposed concentrations was sent to each member of the advisory panels. Each expert selected the concentrations they considered appropriate to deal with all potential clinical scenarios in everyday practice. Consensus was achieved when at least 70% of the respondents agreed on a concentration. This followed a similar methodology to that previously published by groups with similar initiatives. 17 Concentrations selected by less than 40% of the respondents were excluded as it was considered disagreement. Selected concentrations rating between 40% and 70% were subjected to a discussion in the second Delphi round. During this first round, participants could make remarks about specific clinical scenarios and suggest other appropriate concentration values, writing down the new dilutions in the surveys. These new proposed concentrations would be included in the second round. On completion of the first round, all the experts were informed of the results obtained regarding the drugs and concentrations excluded and included in the consensus. Subsequently, the survey comprising the remaining drugs, the initial concentrations, and the new concentration values proposed by the panel members was sent to the participants for the second round, which was carried out between September and December 2020. The same consensus threshold as that used in the first round was applied. However, concentrations not achieving 70% rating were definitively excluded. Discussions between the experts in the first round were conducted virtually via email. In the second round, in addition to email, a virtual meeting was held to debate and compromise on the most controversial scenarios. This work is part of a project that was funded by the Sociedad Española de Farmacia Hospitalaria. Results All expert panel members responded to the survey. Standardisation of intravenous therapy in paediatric patients The number of medicines and concentrations included and selected during the Delphi process are shown in table 1 . The initial proposal included 102 drugs with 192 concentrations to be standardised. Thirty-seven of all drugs (36.3%) were high-risk medications, and 16 (15.7%) were high-risk specifically for paediatric patients. Of all the drugs included, 45 (44.1%) were continuous infusion medications and 59 (57.8%) were intermittent infusions. For two of the drugs (valproic acid and omeprazole), separate concentrations were proposed for continuous infusion and for intermittent infusion. Moreover, for 12 of the drugs analysed (four continuous infusion and eight intermittent infusion), for which a ready-to-administer dosage form was available, the concentrations of the already diluted formulation were subjected to consensus in case there was a need for further dilution. Table 1. Results of the Delphi process for paediatrics and neonatology indications Paediatrics Neonatology Initial proposal Consensus after first round Proposal for second round Final consensus Initial proposal Consensus after first round Proposal for second round Final consensus Number of drugs 102 101 58 101 80 68 54 80 Continuous infusion 45 43 36 44 38 27 32 38 Intermittent infusion 59 58 23 59 43 41 23 43 Number of concentrations 192 132 111 160 189 74 158 120 Continuous infusion 116 70 74 92 121 32 107 74 Intermittent infusion 76 62 37 68 68 42 51 46 Open in a new tab Figure 1 shows the flow diagram of the Delphi process. Tacrolimus was excluded during the first round because its concentration was selected by less than 40% of the respondents. The proposal for the second round included 59 drugs (62% continuous infusion and 38% intermittent infusion) and 111 concentrations (66.7% continuous infusion and 33.3% intermittent infusion). Furthermore, the experts proposed 30 new concentrations for 21 of the continuously infused medicines, of which 57.1% were vasoactive and sedative drugs. Regarding intermittently infused drugs, 24 new concentrations were suggested, of which 64.3% and 12.5% were antimicrobials and anticonvulsants, respectively. Figure 1. Open in a new tab Delphi procedure flow diagram. Eventually, 44 continuous infusion drugs were standardised with a total of 92 concentrations, as well as 59 intermittent infusion medicines with a total of 68 concentrations. It should be noted that there were two drugs that could be administered as a continuous infusion and an intermittent infusion. Thirty-seven of the 101 drugs were high-risk medicines (86.5% continuous infusion and 13.5% intermittent infusion) and 15 were high-risk drugs specifically for paediatric patients (80% intermittent infusion and 20% continuous infusion). The definitive list of the drugs with their agreed-on concentrations is shown in table 2 and online supplemental table 1 . Table 2. List of standardised drugs and concentrations Drug Pediatrics Neonatology Mode of administration High-risk medication Vesicant Acetaminophen* (10 mg/mL, 100 mL vial) 10 mg/mL 10 mg/mL Intermittent infusion High-risk in paediatric applications No Acyclovir sodium (250 mg vial) 5 mg/mL 5 mg/mL Intermittent infusion – Yes Adrenalin bitartrate (1 mg/mL, 1 mL ampoule) 20 μg/ml 20 μg/ml Continuous infusion High-risk medication Yes 40 μg/mL 40 μg/mL 60 μg/ml 60 μg/ml 100 μg/ml Alprostadil (0,5 mg/mL, 1 mL ampoule) 10 μg/mL 5 μg/mL Continuous infusion High-risk medication No 10 μg/mL Alteplase (20 mg vial) 0.2 mg/mL 0.5 mg/mL Continuous infusion High-risk medication No 1 mg/mL Amikacin sulphate (250 mg/mL, 2 mL vial) 5 mg/mL 5 mg/mL Intermittent infusion High-risk in paediatric applications No 10 mg/mL Amiodarone hydrochloride (50 mg/mL, 3 mL ampoule) 2 mg/mL 1 mg/mL Continuous infusion High-risk medication Yes 3 mg/mL 3 mg/mL 6 mg/mL 6 mg/mL Amoxicillin trihydrate/ clavulanate potassium (500/50 mg, vial) 20 mg/mL 20 mg/mL Intermittent infusion – No Amphotericin B liposomal (50 mg vial) 1 mg/mL 1 mg/mL Intermittent infusion High-risk medication No 2 mg/mL Ampicillin sodium (500 mg vial) 100 mg/mL 100 mg/mL Intermittent infusion – No Azithromycin (500 mg vial) 2 mg/mL 2 mg/mL Intermittent infusion – No Aztreonam (1000 mg vial) 20 mg/mL – Intermittent infusion – No Sodium bicarbonate (1 mEq/mL, 10 mL ampoule) 0.5 mEq/mL 0.25 mEq/mL Continuous infusion – Yes 0.5 mEq/mL Caffeine citrate (20 mg/mL, 1 mL ampoule) – 2 mg/mL (caffeine base) Intermittent infusion – No Calcium gluconate (0.46 mEq/mL, 10 mL ampoule) 0.11 mEq/mL 0.23 mEq/mL Continuous infusion – Yes Caspofungin acetate (50 mg vial) 0.5 mg/mL 0.5 mg/mL Intermittent infusion – No Cefazolin sodium (1000 mg vial) 100 mg/mL 25 mg/mL Intermittent infusion – No Cefepime hydrochloride (1000 mg vial) 40 mg/mL 40 mg/mL Intermittent infusion – No Cefotaxime sodium (1000 mg vial) 125 mg/mL 50 mg/mL Intermittent infusion – No Ceftazidime pentahydrate (1000 mg vial) 100 mg/mL 50 mg/mL Intermittent infusion – No Ceftazidime pentahydrate-avibactam sodium (2000/500 mg vial) 20 mg/mL – Intermittent infusion – No Ceftolozane sulfate-tazobactam sodium (1000/500 mg vial) 10 mg/mL – Intermittent infusion – No Ceftriaxone sodium (1000 mg vial) 40 mg/mL 40 mg/mL Intermittent infusion – No Open in a new tab Table continued in online supplemental table 1 . *Ready-to-administer formulation. Supplementary data ejhpharm-2022-003316supp001.pdf (3.2MB, pdf) Standardisation of intravenous therapy in neonates The number of medicines and concentrations included and selected during the Delphi process are shown in table 1 . The initial proposal included 80 drugs with 189 concentrations to be standardised. Thirty-four of all drugs (42.5%) were high-risk medications and 10 (12.5%) were high-risk specifically for neonates. Of all the drugs included, 38 (47.5%) were continuously infused medications whereas 43 (53.5%) were administered through intermittent infusion. For one of the drugs (valproic acid), separate concentrations were proposed for continuous and for intermittent infusion. Moreover, for two of the drugs analysed (one continuous infusion and one intermittent infusion), a ready-to-administer dosage form was available. Figure 1 shows the flow diagram of the Delphi process. In the first round no drug was excluded. The proposal for the second round included 48 drugs (64.6% continuous infusion and 35.4% intermittent infusion) and 103 concentrations (82.5% continuous infusion and 17.5% intermittent infusion). Furthermore, the experts proposed 22 new concentrations for 14 of the continuously infused medicines, 50% of which were sedatives and 28.6% were vasoactive drugs. With regard to intermittently infused drugs, the experts suggested 33 new concentrations for 19 of the medicines, 63.1% of which were antimicrobials. Finally, 38 continuous infusion drugs were standardised with a total of 74 concentrations, and 43 intermittent infusion medicines with a total of 46 concentrations. Thirty-four high-risk medicines were identified (85.3% continuous infusion and 14.7% intermittent infusion) and 12 were high-risk drugs specifically for neonates (83.3% intermittent infusion and 16.7% continuous infusion). Only for one drug (gentamicin 2 mg/mL) was the concentration chosen not the pre-diluted one. This is explained by the fact that several of the experts did not have this specialty available in their centre. The definitive list of the drugs with their agreed-on concentrations is shown in table 2 and online supplemental table 1 . According to the summary of each product characteristics, all the dilutions can be prepared in normal saline or dextrose 5% (the most common diluents in clinical practice), except some drugs that are only compatible in one of these diluents. Drugs that are only compatible in normal saline include alteplase, amoxicillin trihydrate-clavulanate potassium, ampicillin sodium, caspofungin, daptomycin, ertapenem, phenytoin sodium, penicillin G sodium and procainamide hydrochloride. Drugs that are only compatible in dextrose 5% include amiodarone hydrochloride, amphotericin B liposomal, epoprostenol, flecainide acetate, levosimendan and mycophenolate mofetil. All dilutions included were between the concentration range considered stable in the bibliography. 18 Discussion Although standardisation of intravenous therapy has been recognised as a key strategy to improve patient safety, 12 few studies have been undertaken of the problems posed by the variability in the concentrations used for intravenous administration of drugs to paediatric patients and neonates. 19 This is the most comprehensive proposal to date to face the challenge of standardising intravenous therapy in the paediatric setting from a multidisciplinary and nationwide perspective, including a wide-ranging sample of high complexity hospitals specialising in the care of children and neonates. Compared with previous reports on the subject, the present work looks into a larger number of intravenous drugs used in children, both for continuous and intermittent infusions. 13 20 21 The analysis conducted highlights significant differences between the paediatric and neonate populations in terms of the standardisation and consensus-building process. The initial proposal for neonates included a lower number of drugs than that formulated for the paediatric population, mainly because the use of certain medications in neonates is virtually anecdotal. Despite this, the number of drugs included in the proposal for this group of patients is longer than that in other published reports. 20 Mention should be made of the greater difficulty inherent in standardising drug concentrations in the neonate population, given the wide variability of weight encountered and a lower tendency for practitioners to adhere to agreed standards. During the first Delphi procedure a consensus was reached for only 39.2% of concentrations compared with 68.8% for the paediatric population. This could possibly be explained by the fact that practitioners are still unsure about how standardisation may affect the overall amount of fluids administered to neonates, where small weight fluctuations are more impactful than in older and/or heavier patients. This concern, which is shared by numerous paediatricians, has been addressed in a study that showed that standardisation strategies reduce variabilities in the preparation of intravenous medications without a negative impact on the amount of fluid administered. 12 Regarding the administration technique, differences were observed between the consensus on continuously and intermittently infused medicines, both in paediatric patients and neonates. Indeed, a consensus on intermittent infusions was achieved faster and more easily than for continuously infused medicines, probably due to the nature of the administration technique itself and the narrower dose range of these drugs, which significantly limits the number of mixtures that can be used. Moreover, the recommendations regarding optimal dilutions found in the literature for intermittently infused medications are more specific and easier to adapt to the realities of paediatric clinical practice. 22 In this regard it is important to consider that the most formidable challenge to standardisation is the wide variability across drug preparations administered through continuous infusion. Paediatricians have traditionally resorted to the ‘Rule of 3’ or the ‘Rule of 6’, a formula weight-based to calculating to the dose that is required to be delivered and the rate of infusion required to deliver the dose of the vasoactive drug. 23 However, these methods result in an excessive variability in the preparation of continuous infusion doses and it may increase the probability of MEs. Another problem related to the use of formulas for dosing calculations is that they do not take into consideration the lower and upper limits of the concentrations recommended for a drug, particularly in paediatrics. The consensus-building process undertaken as part of this project also showed that variations are particularly apparent in the preparation of vasoactive and sedative drugs. Indeed, most of the additional concentrations proposed by the experts pertained to drugs in these categories. Significant interindividual variations have been found in the response to these types of medications, 24 which requires a widening of the dosing range and may result in significant variations across individual preparations. This greater difficulty in reaching a consensus regarding the standardisation of continuously infused intravenous therapy may be the reason why most initiatives tend to prioritise this administration technique. Indeed, this currently constitutes the greatest challenge in the realm of paediatrics. It must be said that, in its initial phases, the ‘Standardise 4 Safety’ strategy championed by the ASHP 25 prioritised the standardisation of continuous infusion intravenous therapy because of the significant variability across preparations and because most drugs that are continuously infused are high risk. As also shown in the present project, the percentage of continuously infused high-risk drugs reported in the ASHP’s initiative was above 70%. It must be pointed out, however, that this work also includes several drugs considered to be high-risk in the paediatric setting, 8 which are administered through intermittent infusions. The results of this project may be applicable to critical care settings and also across different paediatric and neonatal intermediate care units as the consensus includes a wide range of drugs with indications for a wide variety of clinical situations. Standardisation of intravenous therapy in paediatrics may help lay the foundations for centralised preparations in hospital pharmacy departments 21 and constitute the first step towards implementation of new technologies aimed at preventing MEs, 26 such as the use of smart pumps and barcode administration. In these cases, prior standardisation of intravenous therapy maximises the potential to prevent MEs of these technologies 27 and allows for a more reliable second step process of dosing unit standardisation. Initiatives like the one presented in the present proposal could eventually lead to a generalised adoption of the same set of drug dilution recommendations across all healthcare organisations. This could mean that, in the not too distant future, ready-to-administer dosage forms prepared by the pharmaceutical industry might follow such recommendations, thereby facilitating the treatment of paediatric patients whose needs are not being met by the currently available formulations or their handling requirements. 28 This proposal has not been validated, although the selected concentrations are most like those that each professional could use in their clinical practice. The results of this work are only intended to serve as a support in clinical practice and each organisation may still advocate using their own standard protocols. This document is not mandatory, so it is difficult to be able to validate it in practice. Although some of the Spanish hospitals with the largest number of paediatric beds have participated in this work, it would be necessary to involve scientific societies and health councils to agree on binding national recommendations regarding intravenous standard therapy in paediatric patients. Conclusions Reducing the current variability in the management of intravenous therapy is a strategy pursued by several well-established Spanish and international organisations. This work shows that standardisation with a multidisciplinary consensus is reachable, which may be exported to other centres and eventually help lay the foundations for the formulation of nationwide recommendations that contribute to homogenising paediatric clinical practice. Footnotes X @ceciliamfll, @aherranza Collaborators: Standardization Intravenous Drug Work Group: Standardization Intravenous Drug Work Group: Amelia Caridad Sánchez Galindo, Marta Cordero, Ana Belén García-Moreno, Maria Arriaga Redondo, Maria de la Sierra Lara, Ana María Llorente de la Fuente, Sonia Gil Domínguez, Tania Carbayo, Carmen Peña, Carmen García Muñoz, Jóse Camacho Alonso, María Rivas Marquez, Regina Abujeta Soria, Carmen Gallego Fernández, Antonio Postigo Jiménez, Jose Manuel Gordillo López, Lucía Yunquera Romero; Marta Muro Brussi, Sergio Moreno Jaquete, Belén Rodríguez Marrodan, Julia Pérez Gil, Daniel Moreno Leira, Jose Francisco Vázquez Castelos, Soledad Martínez Regueira, Yolanda Sánchez Suárez, Cristina Martínez Roca; Susana Clemente Bautista, Francisca Munar Bauza, Maria Jose Cabañas Poy, César Ruiz Campillo, Alba Quesada Vargas, Aida Felipe Villalobos, Montserrat Fontanals Caravaca, Sara Palou Camps, Cristina Carrasco Carrasco, Tania Rivera Dominguez, Maria Goretti Lopez Ramos, Andrés Concha Torre, Susana Pérez González, Enrique García López, Lorena Busto Parada, Juan Francisco Collado Caparrós, Joaquín García Ferreira, Juan Jurado Bellón, Francisco Javier Cano Padilla, Jesús David Pastor Rodríguez, Beatriz Garrido Corro, Jon Igartua Araudogoitia, David González Pérez, Mª Isabel Larburu Aristicabal, Mónica Valerón Lemaur, Mª Dolores Quintana Santana; Raquel Aguilar Salmerón, Alberto Trujillo Fagundo, Pablo Álvarez Montañana, Sonia Garcés Sánchez, Vicente García Cárcel, Isabel Font Noguera, María José Borrás Vaño, Ana Alejandra García Robles, Montserrat Nieto Moro, Margarita Cuervas-Mons Vendrell, Elia Sánchez Valderrabanos, Francisco Javier Portero Prados, Maria Antonia Pérez Moreno, Daniel Fernández Sánchez, Concha Álvarez del Vayo.Supervision: Silvia Manrique-Rodríguez, Cecilia M Fernández-Llamazares, Ana Herranz-Alonso, María Sanjurjo-Sáez. Contributors: Conceptualisation and planning: SMR, CMF-L, IT-S. Designing the method and participating in the expert panel: SMR, CMF-L, IT-S. Collecting and analysis of the results: SMR, CMF-L, IT-S. Writing – original draft: IT-S, SMR. Writing – review and editing: SMR, CMF-L, AH-A, MS-S. Guarantor: SMR. Competing interests: None declared. Provenance and peer review: Not commissioned; externally peer reviewed. Supplemental material: This content has been supplied by the author(s). It has not been vetted by BMJ Publishing Group Limited (BMJ) and may not have been peer-reviewed. Any opinions or recommendations discussed are solely those of the author(s) and are not endorsed by BMJ. BMJ disclaims all liability and responsibility arising from any reliance placed on the content. Where the content includes any translated material, BMJ does not warrant the accuracy and reliability of the translations (including but not limited to local regulations, clinical guidelines, terminology, drug names and drug dosages), and is not responsible for any error and/or omissions arising from translation and adaptation or otherwise. Data availability statement Data sharing not applicable as no datasets generated and/or analysed for this study. Ethics statements Patient consent for publication Not applicable. Ethics approval Not applicable. References 1. World Health Organization . Patient safety monograph, 2019. Available: https://www.who.int/es/news-room/fact-sheets/detail/patient-safety 2. Aranaz-Andrés JM, Aibar-Remón C, Vitaller-Burillo J, et al. Impact and preventability of adverse events in Spanish public hospitals: results of the Spanish National Study of Adverse Events (ENEAS). Int J Qual Health Care 2009;21:408–14. 10.1093/intqhc/mzp047 [ DOI ] [ PubMed ] [ Google Scholar ] 3. Ghaleb MA, Barber N, Franklin BD, et al. Systematic review of medication errors in pediatric patients. Ann Pharmacother 2006;40:1766–76. 10.1345/aph.1G717 [ DOI ] [ PubMed ] [ Google Scholar ] 4. Stockwell DC, Landrigan CP, Toomey SL, et al. Adverse events in hospitalized pediatric patients. Pediatrics 2018;142:e20173360. 2018. 10.1542/peds.2017-3360 [ DOI ] [ PMC free article ] [ PubMed ] [ Google Scholar ] 5. Wong ICK, Ghaleb MA, Franklin BD, et al. Incidence and nature of dosing errors in paediatric medications: a systematic review. Drug Saf 2004;27:661–70. 10.2165/00002018-200427090-00004 [ DOI ] [ PubMed ] [ Google Scholar ] 6. Garrido-Corro B, Fernández-Llamazares CM, Rodríguez-Marrodán B, et al. Estudio multicéntrico de la incidencia y evitabilidad de los incidentes por medicamentos en pacientes que acuden a los servicios de urgencias pediátricas. Farm Hosp 2021;45:115–20. 10.7399/fh.11583 [ DOI ] [ PubMed ] [ Google Scholar ] 7. Sutherland A, Canobbio M, Clarke J, et al. Incidence and prevalence of intravenous medication errors in the UK: a systematic review. Eur J Hosp Pharm 2020;27:3–8. 10.1136/ejhpharm-2018-001624 [ DOI ] [ PMC free article ] [ PubMed ] [ Google Scholar ] 8. Cotrina Luque J, Guerrero Aznar MD, Alvarez de